# The web tier's collector, baked into the launcher for the same reason as worker.options: the
# image's AOT cache is trained through this launcher and maps only under the collector it was
# trained with. AotCacheOptionsSpec holds the overlays to it.
-XX:+UseG1GC
# Map the AOT cache at its own base instead of a random one: relocating it patches every pointer and
# dirties every page, so the whole archive became private memory (worker image, PL options: 741 MB
# anonymous RSS relocated, 638 MB in place). Diagnostic, so unlocked first: web's JAVA_OPTS do not
# unlock diagnostics, and AotCacheOptionsSpec starts a JVM under these options alone.
-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions
-XX:ArchiveRelocationMode=0
# Cap the temporary direct buffers NIO caches per thread: a socket read into a heap buffer goes
# through a direct one of the same size, kept per thread and unbounded by default. NMT's "Other" held
# 161 MB of them on worker-uk (~75 buffers of ~2 MB, the Mongo driver's replies) until this cap.
-Djdk.nio.maxCachedBufferSize=262144
# Share the byte arrays of equal Strings the collector keeps: 57.9 MB of worker-pl's heap was
# duplicate contents ("US", "2D", titles, JSON values); 11 MB off the live old generation measured.
-XX:+UseStringDeduplication
# G1's heap-sizing ergonomics as JDK 25 ran them (JDK 27's G1Arguments overrides each when left at its
# default: UseCondCardMark on, GCTimeRatio 24, Min/MaxHeapFreeRatio 0/100). Kept from the JDK 25 parity
# file when the rest of it was given back (2026-10-23): the web tier's G1 under 27's values is
# unmeasured. Inert for resizing while Xms == Xmx; LauncherJvmFlagsSpec holds the web JVM to them.
-XX:-UseCondCardMark
-XX:GCTimeRatio=12
-XX:MinHeapFreeRatio=40
-XX:MaxHeapFreeRatio=70
